// Biome is the formatter and the linter. It replaces oxlint, and it adds the
// half this repo never had: there was no formatter at all.
//
// WHY IT CHANGED, AND IT IS NOT THAT BIOME IS NICER. `docs/PLAN.md` Phase 6
// moved this project off Astro, and the tooling went first because of what
// oxlint could not see. Measured at the time with an identical
// duplicate-declaration in three files: oxlint reported it in `.ts`, reported it
// in `.svelte`, and said NOTHING for `.astro`.
//
// That was **5,531 lines across 17 files with no lint coverage at all** — forty
// per cent of the view layer, unchecked, while `bun run lint` reported success.
// SU-SRD hit the same thing and recorded the same number for its own Astro
// migration (2,034 lines across 17 files).
//
// Those files are gone now, and the view layer is `.tsx` that Biome lints like
// everything else. The config stays because the formatter choices below were
// measured against this repository and remain correct; the blind spot that
// forced the change is simply no longer the reason to keep it.
//
// THE SETTINGS BELOW ARE THIS REPO'S EXISTING STYLE, MEASURED RATHER THAN
// IMPORTED. Copying SU-SRD's Biome config verbatim would have reformatted
// every file in the repository, because that project writes single quotes and
// omits semicolons. Counted over 17,023 lines of source here: 7,071 double
// quotes against 97 single, semicolons throughout, and a 90th-percentile line
// length of 79. So: double, always, 80. The point of adopting a formatter now
// is to stop future diffs arguing about whitespace, not to open one enormous
// argument about it today.
{
  "$schema": "./node_modules/@biomejs/biome/configuration_schema.json",
  "root": true,
  // BIOME DOES NOT READ `.gitignore` UNLESS YOU ASK IT TO, and the negations in
  // `files.includes` below were an enumeration of the cases someone had already
  // been bitten by rather than of the rule.
  //
  // Measured on a checkout that predates `#107` deleting Astro: the leftover
  // `apps/site/.astro/` — ignored by git, so `git status` is clean and nothing
  // in review can see it — takes `bun run lint` AND `bun run format:check` to
  // exit 1 from the repo root. Four lint errors and three unformatted files, in
  // generated Astro type stubs for a framework this project no longer has.
  //
  // CI cannot catch this class of failure by construction: it checks out fresh,
  // so every ignored path is absent there. It only ever fires locally, where the
  // fix looks like "the linter is broken" rather than "delete this directory".
  //
  // `!.claude` stays below rather than being folded into this. `.gitignore` has
  // `.claude/worktrees/`, so this line does cover it — but the failure that line
  // documents is a nested-root-config error raised during the discovery walk,
  // and belt-and-braces is worth one line for the one case that hard-errors.
  "vcs": {
    "enabled": true,
    "clientKind": "git",
    "useIgnoreFile": true
  },
  "files": {
    "ignoreUnknown": true,
    "includes": [
      "apps/**",
      "packages/**",
      "scripts/**",
      // Agent worktrees are full checkouts, each carrying its own copy of this
      // file with `"root": true`, and Biome 2.5+ hard-errors on a nested root
      // config. The discovery walk is independent of the includes above, so
      // without this line `bun run lint` exits 1 from the repo root for anyone
      // who has ever used the worktree workflow — which is how all of this
      // project's agent work is done. CI would never have seen it.
      "!.claude",
      "!**/node_modules",
      "!**/dist",
      "!**/coverage",
      // Committed build artefacts. Formatting them would make the checked-in
      // copy differ from what the generator emits, which is the one thing
      // their drift checks exist to catch.
      "!data",
      "!**/*-snapshots"
    ]
  },
  "formatter": {
    "enabled": true,
    "indentStyle": "space",
    "indentWidth": 2,
    "lineWidth": 80,
    "lineEnding": "lf"
  },
  "javascript": {
    "formatter": {
      "quoteStyle": "double",
      "semicolons": "always",
      "trailingCommas": "all",
      "arrowParentheses": "always"
    }
  },
  "linter": {
    "enabled": true,
    "rules": {
      "preset": "recommended",
      // The three oxlint categories this repo ran at "error" were correctness,
      // suspicious and perf. Biome's recommended set covers correctness and
      // suspicious; these are the specific rules that CAUGHT SOMETHING here
      // during stack E and would otherwise be lost in the swap.
      "suspicious": {
        "noExplicitAny": "error"
      },
      "correctness": {
        "noUnusedVariables": "error",
        "noUnusedImports": "error"
      },
      "complexity": {
        // OFF, BECAUSE THE BRACKETS ARE CARRYING INFORMATION HERE.
        //
        // All 36 findings were `card.legality["ll_restricted_affects_full_cycle"]`
        // and its siblings: UPSTREAM's field names, snake_case, read off types
        // that are index signatures (`CardLegality` is a
        // `Record<string, …>`). This repo states in several places that
        // upstream's names are "kept verbatim", and the bracket is the visual
        // mark of a key we did not choose and cannot rename.
        //
        // Rewriting them as `.ll_restricted_affects_full_cycle` is 36 edits
        // that make the code marginally shorter and lose the one signal
        // distinguishing a foreign key from one of ours.
        "useLiteralKeys": "off"
      },
      "style": {
        // OFF, AND EVERY REMAINING USE IS GUARDED ONE LINE ABOVE ITSELF.
        //
        // The shape is `children.length === 1 ? children[0]! : …`, where the
        // ternary's own condition is the proof the index exists and TypeScript
        // cannot narrow through it. Writing a fallback there would mean
        // inventing a value for a branch that cannot be reached, which is a
        // worse claim than the assertion.
        //
        // This is not a licence for `!` generally — see `pages/card.page.tsx`,
        // which writes the unreachable branch out longhand precisely because a
        // `!` there "would be a claim about another module's invariant". The
        // rule cannot tell those two cases apart; a reader can.
        "noNonNullAssertion": "off"
      }
    }
  },
  "assist": {
    "actions": {
      "source": {
        // OFF, AND IT WAS ON FOR ABOUT TEN MINUTES.
        //
        // It sorts imports alphabetically and hoists them above whatever sits
        // between them — which in this repo is the file's explanatory docblock.
        // Applied once, it moved `import CardEntry` above the 35-line header
        // of the card page, leaving the comment that explains the file stranded
        // underneath an import statement. Several other files the same way.
        //
        // In a codebase where the prose IS most of the value, a formatter that
        // relocates prose to sort identifiers has the trade backwards. Import
        // order here is maintained by hand and has never been the problem.
        "organizeImports": "off"
      }
    }
  },
  "overrides": [
    {
      // A TEST TABLE OVER HETEROGENEOUS COMPONENTS IS NOT TYPABLE, and that is
      // the whole of this exemption. `a11y.test.ts` walks every component in
      // the library through one `render(component, { props })` call; the
      // components have different prop types by definition, so the parameter
      // is either `any` or a fourteen-arm union that has to be edited whenever
      // a component is added — a union whose only function would be to be
      // widened. `FiligreeCorner.stories.ts` is the same shape.
      //
      // `noExplicitAny` stays an ERROR in source, where it reports nothing.
      "includes": ["**/*.test.ts", "**/*.stories.ts"],
      "linter": {
        "rules": {
          "suspicious": {
            "noExplicitAny": "off"
          }
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
